\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Exact covariance on the alternating four-cycle}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Take the [four-cycle](fdip-0003) on #{V=(0,1,2,3)}, with forward
edges and weights #{(1,t,1,t)}, #{t>0}. Use counting pairing and
#{Q=L+m^2I}, #{m>0}. Put #{a=m^2} and #{s=1+t+a}.
The following functions on vertices form an orthonormal eigenbasis;
the middle column lists the unnormalized rows #{2u_i^T}:
##{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
i&2u_i^T&\lambda_i\\\hline
0&(1,1,1,1)&0\\
1&(1,-1,-1,1)&2\\
2&(1,1,-1,-1)&2t\\
3&(1,-1,1,-1)&2+2t
\end{array}}}
\proof{
\p{Each row has squared norm four. Distinct rows agree in two signs
and disagree in two, giving inner product zero. The weighted coordinate
formula is #{(L\phi)_0=(1+t)\phi_0-\phi_1-t\phi_3}, with the other
rows given by their adjacent weights. For #{v_i=2u_i}, all four products
are
##{\begin{aligned}
Lv_0&=(0,0,0,0),\\
Lv_1&=(2,-2,-2,2),\\
Lv_2&=(2t,2t,-2t,-2t),\\
Lv_3&=(2+2t,-2-2t,\\
 &\qquad 2+2t,-2-2t).
\end{aligned}}
For example the zeroth coordinates are respectively #{0},
#{(1+t)+1-t=2}, #{(1+t)-1+t=2t}, and #{(1+t)+1+t=2+2t};
the same substitutions at the other three vertices give the displayed
signs. Thus #{Lu_i=\lambda_i u_i}. This basis remains orthonormal at
#{t=1}, when #{\lambda_1=\lambda_2}; no distinct-eigenvalue assumption
is needed.}
}
\p{Set
##{\begin{aligned}
q_0&=a,\\
q_1&=a+2,\\
q_2&=a+2t,\\
q_3&=a+2+2t,\\
\Delta&=a(a+2)(a+2t)(a+2+2t).
\end{aligned}}
All four #{q_i} are positive. With #{P_{u_i}=u_i u_i^T}, [normalization](fdip-002T) and
[inverse covariance](fdip-000B) give
##{\begin{aligned}
\det Q&=\Delta,& Z_Q&=4\pi^2/\sqrt\Delta,\\
G&=\sum_{i=0}^3q_i^{-1}P_{u_i}.
\end{aligned}}
With #{O} the array whose columns are #{u_i}, this is
#{G=O\operatorname{diag}(q_i^{-1})O^T}.}
\p{Write #{d_i=q_i^{-1}}. Multiplying the signs of the relevant
coordinates in each projector gives
##{\begin{aligned}
4G_{00}&=d_0+d_1+d_2+d_3,\\
4G_{01}&=d_0-d_1+d_2-d_3,\\
4G_{02}&=d_0-d_1-d_2+d_3,\\
4G_{03}&=d_0+d_1-d_2-d_3.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the complete covariance, with #{(A,B,C,D)=(G_{00},G_{01},G_{02},G_{03})}, is
##{G=\begin{pmatrix}
A&B&C&D\\B&A&D&C\\C&D&A&B\\D&C&B&A
\end{pmatrix}.}
In particular the variance and adjacent two-point expectation are the
first two formulas, with their indicated signs.}
\p{For the opposite entry, the common denominator #{4\Delta} has
numerator
##{\begin{aligned}
N&=(a+2)(a+2t)(a+2+2t)\\
 &\quad-a(a+2t)(a+2+2t)\\
 &\quad-a(a+2)(a+2+2t)\\
 &\quad+a(a+2)(a+2t)\\
 &=2[(a+2t)(a+2+2t)-a(a+2)]\\
 &=8t(a+1+t).
\end{aligned}}
Consequently #{G_{02}=2ts/\Delta}. At #{(t,m)=(1,1)},
#{q=(1,3,3,5)}, #{\Delta=45}, #{Z_Q=4\pi^2/(3\sqrt5)}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
&(G_{00},G_{01},G_{02},G_{03})\\
&\qquad=(7/15,1/5,2/15,1/5).
\end{aligned}}
At #{(t,m)=(2,1)}, #{q=(1,3,5,7)}, #{\Delta=105}, and
##{(G_{00},G_{01},G_{02},G_{03})
  =(44,19,16,26)/105.}
These numbers are obtained by direct substitution in the four signed
sums above. They give, in particular, the opposite covariance used in
[block elimination](fdip-000F) and the two distinct adjacent covariances
used in [relabeling](fdip-000Y).}
\p{More generally, weights #{(b,c,b,c)} with #{b,c>0} keep the same
four eigenvectors and give eigenvalues #{0,2b,2c,2b+2c}, by the same
coordinate products. This is a real spectral calculation. The
[arithmetic columns](fdip-000U) are #{2u_i}, whose Gram matrix is #{4I};
they are not an orthonormal basis over an arbitrary coefficient ring.}
\p{The eigenvectors and signed sums are computed here from the weighted
cycle.}
\p{\citet{Section A.5.2, pp. 646-647}{boyd2004convex} gives general
spectral decomposition.}
\p{\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical} gives the
Gaussian density. Covariance equals inverse precision by \ref{fdip-000B}.}
